                        HOL.

Validation assets:

The conformance corpora, recorded solver proofs, coverage reports, and their
validation tools live in the separately versioned `holsmt-validation` dependency, not in this source tree. The `holsmt-validation` repository is available at https://github.com/lukaszcz/holsmt-validation.git.

The commands below assume `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR` points at the check-out of `holsmt-validation`; see that repository's README for the dependency contract and standalone validation instructions.

User contract:

HolSmt targets SMT-LIB 2.7.  The generated support report
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/SMTLIB_COVERAGE.md` is the source of truth for parser,
typechecker, translation, solver and proof-replay status.  Its support rows are
checked against `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/coverage_manifest.json`; rows marked
as tested support carry manifest-derived test IDs, and unsupported rows carry
diagnostic test IDs.  Regenerate it from the repository root with:

  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/generate_smtlib_coverage.py

Release/CI checks that want to forbid unresolved coverage can use:

  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/generate_smtlib_coverage.py --release

The SMT-LIB 2.7 frontend is not a full-language completeness claim.  It
accepts the commands, theories and logics recorded in the matrix, and it must
fail with an explicit invalid-input or unsupported diagnostic for accepted
syntax whose proof reconstruction is not implemented.  SMT-LIB 3 is not part
of the contract: no SMT-LIB 3 parser, logic registry or replay path is
currently identified in the matrix.

`Z3_TAC` is the checked Z3 tactic.  It invokes Z3 in proof-producing mode,
parses the proof, replays supported proof rules in HOL, and rejects theorem
outputs with unexpected oracle tags.  It has no oracle fallback in the complete
target: `UNSAT NONE` from the generic SMT tactic is not an acceptable
`Z3_TAC` result.  Use `Z3_ORACLE_TAC` only when an oracle result is intended;
it is outside the checked `Z3_TAC` trusted-code-base boundary.

`CVC_TAC` is the checked cvc5 tactic.  Its default proof format is cvc5 CPC
with `--proof-granularity=dsl-rewrite`; the CPC parser version-gates every
rule and the replay engine reconstructs a HOL theorem before `CVC.sml` checks
its hypotheses, conclusion, and oracle tags.  Set `HOL4_CVC_EXECUTABLE` to
select cvc5.  CPC is the sole checked cvc5 replay format.

For a cvc5 upgrade, capture the generated post-preprocessing queries and run
the checked matrix rather than accepting proof-format drift.  The pinned cvc5
evidence and commands are in
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/cvc5-version-matrix.md`.

Supported Z3 proof dialect and tested anchors:

  Proof dialect      Proof options used by HolSmt                  Support status
  Z3 4.x             proof=true pp.simplify_implies=false          v4 proof path

The configured executable comes from `HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE`.  Proof parse and
replay diagnostics include the discovered Z3 version and command stem.
The checked-in proof corpus, local contract, and version verifier use the exact
tested anchors 4.11.2, 4.12.4, 4.13.0, 4.14.1, and 4.15.3.  The verifier writes its five-version report to
`.holsmt-z3-version-matrix/report.json` in the validation checkout.

Seq, Set, and Bag are solver extensions rather than SMT-LIB standard
theories.  The coverage matrix at
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/SMTLIB_COVERAGE.md` records every
supported operator, solver, and version: Z3 4.11.2--4.15.x has array-based
Sets and Z3-only sequence higher-order operators; cvc5 1.3.4 has native
finite `set.*`/`bag.*` and its cvc5-only sequence operations.  In particular,
`seq.fold_left` is unavailable on Z3 4.11.2 and available from 4.12.4.

Z3 4.14.1 rejects `Array` under seven non-QF logic names that HolSmt can infer:
`ALIRA`, `ANIA`, `ANIRA`, `AUFDTLIA`, `AUFDTLIRA`, `AUFDTNIRA`, and
`AUFBVDT`.  For every discovered 4.14.* patch version, HolSmt therefore widens
an array-bearing query in exactly those logics to the sound `ALL` superset.
Generic SMT-LIB inference and neighboring Z3 minor versions keep their original
logic.  Z3 4.14.1 is the exact tested anchor for this minor-series runtime
policy.
Use `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/verify_z3_versions.sh` to download representative Z3
releases, run the proof-corpus gate, checked nonlinear replay smoke test and
full `src/HolSmt/selftest.exe` checks, and record the exact versions verified
in a JSON report.  Full nonlinear real replay checks require CSDP (`coinor-csdp`, or
`HOL4_CSDP_EXECUTABLE`) just as the Docker CI jobs do.
On platforms without trustworthy compatible Z3 release binaries, use
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/build_z3_versions.sh` to build representative source
releases locally with the TPTP frontend and install versioned binaries under
`~/.local/bin`.

Unsupported or deprecated Z3 versions:

  Version class              Reason
  All pre-4 versions         Legacy 2.x/3.x proof support has been removed
                             from the contract.  Only Z3 4.x is supported.
  Unlisted Z3 4.x releases   Outside the exact tested anchor set.  A patch
                             release of an anchor counts as tested: an anchor
                             stands for its whole major.minor series.  The
                             contract script fails fast on the rest unless
                             --allow-unsupported-z3 is passed.
  Future major versions      Unsupported until the proof command, proof-rule
                             registry, corpus gate and matrix are updated.
  Undiscoverable version     Diagnostics still run, but the version cannot be
                             matched to an exact tested anchor or the 4.14.*
                             runtime policy.

None of these classes is a replay failure.  An unsupported or undiscoverable
version is never rejected: `Z3_Proof.resolve_version`, and its cvc5
counterpart `CPC_Proof.resolve_version`, warn once and then replay under the
tested version nearest the discovered one -- the anchor sharing its
major.minor series where there is one, otherwise the nearest anchor by version
order, or the latest anchor when the version could not be discovered at all.
Resolution happens once, as the proof is parsed, so rule gating and
diagnostics downstream always see a tested version.  The anchor set it draws
on is `Z3_Proof.supported_z3_versions` (`CPC_Proof.supported_cvc_versions` for
cvc5); keep it in sync with the table above.

SMT-LIB support matrix:

  Area                         Parse  Typecheck  HOL translate  Replay
  Core Bool/UF                 yes    yes        native         Z3/cvc5 paths
  Int/Real arithmetic          yes    yes        native         linear and nonlinear
  Fixed bit-vectors            yes    yes        native         partial
  ArraysEx select/store        yes    yes        native         yes
  Unicode strings              yes    yes        native         Z3/cvc5 paths
  Regular expressions          yes    yes        native         Z3/cvc5 paths
  FloatingPoint                yes    yes        native smtfp;  Z3/cvc5 checked;
                                                proved kit      D2/D12 gates
  Datatypes                    yes    yes        native         yes
  Recursive/mutual/parametric
    datatypes                  yes    yes        native         yes
  Datatype match               yes    yes        native         yes
  HO-Core function sorts       yes    yes        dual dialect   Z3/cvc5 paths
  Lambda/application surface   yes    yes        dual dialect   Z3/cvc5 paths
  Partial application          yes    yes        dual dialect   Z3/cvc5 paths
  Sequences (Seq) yes yes native HOL lists; Z3/cvc5 checked
                         Z3 HO only; `seq.fold_left` needs Z3 >= 4.12.4
  Sets (Set)      yes yes native predicates; Z3 Array dialect
                         cvc5 finite `set.*` guard; checked
  Bags (Bag)      yes yes native HOL bags; Z3 Array Int + `(_ map ...)`
                         cvc5 finite `bag.*` guard; checked

Unicode-string and regular-expression replay covers goals stated over the
native `smtstr` carrier.  A goal stated over HOL's `:string` reaches the
solver only as a `str_inj` image, and Z3 then answers it with seq th-lemmas
over that image while cvc5 uses the `trust` and `str-substr-full-eq` CPC
rules; none of those replay, so such goals are supported on the oracle
tactics only.

FloatingPoint replay covers goals stated over the canonical-NaN `smtfp`
carrier.  Native `binary_ieee` goals reach the SMT FloatingPoint sort only
through the proved `smtfp_intro` transfer kit.  The kit covers record
literals, binders, special values, classification and sign predicates,
abs/neg, comparisons, and the native add/sub/mul/div/sqrt/fma operations.
It does not claim that raw `float` record equality is SMT FloatingPoint
equality: any residual raw-float term is emitted with an uninterpreted sort,
never an FP sort.  Checked Z3 and cvc5 replay covers the corpus-demonstrated
part of this surface; unsupported symbolic shapes are named D2 gates and
over-budget bit-blasts are named D12 gates.

Logic coverage summary:

  Logic family                 Contract summary
  UF, QF_UF                   Implemented parser, translation, solving and
                              checked replay coverage.
  LIA, NIA, UFIDL, UFLIA,     Implemented integer parser/translator/replay,
  UFNIA, QF_IDL, QF_LIA,      coverage; parser is permissive and does not
  QF_NIA, QF_UFIDL, QF_UFLIA  enforce every linear or difference-logic
                              fragment restriction. Nonlinear replay uses the
                              SOS ladder where needed.
  LRA, NRA, UFLRA, UFNRA,     Implemented real parser/translator/replay,
  QF_LRA, QF_NRA, QF_RDL,     coverage; parser is permissive about fragment
  QF_UFLRA, QF_UFNRA          restrictions. Some nonlinear real replay cases
                              require CSDP as a certificate finder.
  QF_LIRA, QF_NIRA,           Implemented mixed Int/Real coverage for current
  QF_UFLIRA, QF_UFNIRA        selftests and dictionaries.
  BV, UFBV, QF_ABV,           Implemented bit-vector parser/translator/replay
  QF_AUFBV, QF_BV, QF_UFBV    coverage, with division/remainder/overflow edges
                              tracked in the soundness-audit matrix.
  ALIA, ANIA, AUFLIA,         Core + Ints + ArraysEx dictionaries; parser
  QF_ALIA, QF_ANIA,           coverage is implemented, while solver/replay
  QF_AUFLIA, QF_AUFNIA        status is matrix-specific and not a blanket
                              completeness claim.
  ALIRA, ANIRA, AUFLIRA,      Core + Reals_Ints + ArraysEx dictionaries; parser
  AUFNIRA, QF_ALRA,           coverage is implemented, while solver/replay
  QF_ANRA, QF_AUFLIRA,        status is matrix-specific.
  QF_AUFNIRA
  UFDT, QF_UFDT,              Core + Datatypes dictionaries, including
  UFDTLIA, UFDTNIA,           recursive, mutual and parametric datatypes;
  QF_UFDTLIA, QF_UFDTNIA      parser/typechecker, native translation and
                              checked replay are covered in driver modes.
  UFDTLIRA, UFDTNIRA,         Core + Datatypes + Int/Real dictionaries;
  QF_UFDTLIRA                 mixed numeric fragment restrictions are
                              enforced by the manifest-backed logic rows.
  AUFDTLIA, AUFDTLIRA,        ArraysEx + Datatypes + arithmetic dictionaries;
  AUFDTNIRA, UFBVDT,          supported non-FP DT logic family rows are in the
  AUFBVDT, AUFBVDTLIA,        default complete-conformance suite with
  AUFBVDTNIA, AUFBVDTNIRA     fragment-violation diagnostics.
  QF_AX                       Array parser/typechecker coverage exists; proof
                              reconstruction coverage remains limited.
  QF_S, QF_SLIA, QF_SNIA      Native Unicode string and RegLan translation;
                              checked Z3 seq/char/regex replay and cvc5 CPC
                              string replay are manifest-backed.
  QF_FP, QF_FPBV, QF_BVFP,    Native smtfloatTheory translation and checked
  QF_UFFP, QF_UFBVFP          fpa2bv replay.  Ground evaluation and Tier-2
                              shapes replay; staged symbolic arithmetic is
                              either checked by the add/sub/mul circuit rung
                              or reported as an enumerated D2/D12 gate.
  HO_ALL, HO_UF, HO_QF_UF,    cvc5-convention extension logics for the SMT-LIB
  HO_UFLIA, HO_QF_UFLIA,      2.7 HO-Core surface.  Parser/typechecker and
  HO_UFLRA, HO_QF_UFLRA,      Standard27 cvc5-oracle modes are covered; checked
  HO_AUFLIA, HO_AUFLIRA,      replay uses Z3LambdaArray for Z3 and CPC for
                              cvc5.
  HO_QF_AUFLIA                These are not official SMT-LIB logic names.
  ALL                         HolSmt's permissive aggregate parser dictionary,
                              not an official SMT-LIB logic and not a
                              soundness/completeness claim.
  SMT-LIB 3 logics            Unsupported; no SMT-LIB 3 registry is present.

For the complete per-logic and per-theory rows, including evidence, test IDs,
diagnostic IDs and test status, read
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/SMTLIB_COVERAGE.md`.  Public documentation should not
claim more than that generated report.

The `SmtLib_Parser` library default remains placeholder datatype mode unless a
caller sets `elaborate_datatypes = true`.  Placeholder mode installs datatype
sorts and symbols without elaborating genuine HOL datatypes, and rejects
binding `match` patterns with a precise diagnostic.  The command-line
typecheck and checked Z3 tactic drivers use native datatype elaboration by
default; `holsmt-typecheck --placeholder-datatypes` keeps the library default
available as an explicit gate.

HOL translation records include HOLTheoryEncoding rows for the advanced
theories.  These rows distinguish native SMT-LIB encodings from conservative
parser/typechecker embeddings and record proof obligations for the soundness
audit.  Replay is not claimed merely because parsing, typechecking or HOL
translation succeeds.

Trusted-code-base boundary and soundness audit:

This section documents the checked boundaries for `Z3_TAC` and `CVC_TAC`. It
is an audit guide, not a completeness claim. The generated coverage report
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/SMTLIB_COVERAGE.md` contains the cross-referenced
support and semantic-mismatch matrix. The report is generated from
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/smtlib_coverage.json` plus manifest-backed evidence
in `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/coverage_manifest.json`; tested support rows must
carry executable test IDs, and unsupported rows must carry diagnostic test IDs.

Checked `Z3_TAC` path:

`Z3_TAC` is `HolSmtLib.GENERIC_SMT_TAC Z3.Z3_SMT_Prover`.

For a theorem-producing result, the trusted path is:

- `SmtLib.sml`: translates the HOL goal to SMT-LIB and records the selected
  logic, emitted symbols and HOL theory encodings.
- `smtstringTheory` fixes the HOL definitions used for the SMT-LIB
  UnicodeStrings and RegLan meanings.  SMT strings use the dedicated HOL
  carrier `smtstr`, a type definition over the code-point-bounded `num`
  lists, so the SMT `String` sort is distinct from ordinary HOL lists and its
  HOL universe is exactly the SMT-LIB String universe: `wfstr` holds of every
  `:smtstr`, so quantifiers need no wellformedness relativization in either
  direction.  HOL checks those definitions and all derivative, matching, and
  correspondence theorems; the trusted translation choice is that these
  definitions faithfully represent the SMT-LIB theory.
- `SmtLib_String_Literal.sml` decodes input literals to code-point payloads,
  wraps them with `SmtStr`, and encodes native string literals for the
  printer.  The decoder/encoder and their choice of SMT-LIB escape
  representation are trusted translation code, with deterministic round-trip
  and range checks in `Unittest.sml`.
- Native HOL strings are first rewritten through the proved `str_inj`
  injectivity and operator-transfer theorems.  Each resulting injection image
  is generalized to an unconstrained SMT-LIB `String` value.  A solver result
  proved for every well-formed Unicode string therefore specializes soundly
  to the injection image, while tactic validation carries the exact HOL-side
  rewrite back to the original goal.  This is the only route from HOL string
  content to the SMT `String` sort: HOL's `:string` (a `char list`) is not
  mapped to `String`, and a `:string` term that survives the rewrite is
  emitted as an uninterpreted sort.  There is therefore no HOL char-list
  versus SMT-LIB Unicode-string semantic mismatch left to audit.
- `SmtLib_Datatypes.sml`: elaborates SMT-LIB datatype declarations to HOL
  datatypes and builds selector/tester case expressions.
- `Z3.sml`: invokes the configured Z3 executable, requests proof output,
  parses only `unsat` proof-producing results and calls
  `Library.check_oracle_tags` on the reconstructed theorem.
- `Z3_ProofParser.sml`: parses Z3 proof terms through the versioned proof-rule
  registry and rejects unknown rules.
- `Z3_ProofReplay.sml`: replays supported proof rules in HOL and reports
  unsupported theory-lemma families with explicit diagnostics.
- `smtstringz3Theory` gives reverse-engineered meanings to Z3's undocumented
  `seq.*`, `char.*`, and `aut.accept` proof symbols.  The meanings are derived
  from the versioned proof corpus, but every accepted emitted clause is
  re-proved from HOL definitions and the proved derivative correspondence;
  no reverse-engineered clause is assumed.  Unknown shapes fail explicitly,
  while approved solver limitations remain enumerated D2 diagnostics.
- HOL kernel, primitive inference rules, accepted HOL theories and trusted
  external process execution are part of the surrounding HOL4 TCB.

`Z3_ORACLE_TAC` and `Z3.Z3_SMT_Oracle` are outside this checked boundary. The
generic tactic's `UNSAT NONE` oracle branch is not an acceptable result for
`Z3_TAC`; `Z3.Z3_SMT_Prover` must return `UNSAT (SOME thm)` after proof replay.

Checked `CVC_TAC` path:

`CVC_TAC` is `HolSmtLib.GENERIC_SMT_TAC CVC.CVC_SMT_Prover`. Its default cvc5
proof format is CPC at `dsl-rewrite` granularity.

- `CVC.sml` records the original goal, invokes cvc5 in CPC mode, validates the
  reconstructed theorem against that goal, and rejects oracle tags.
- `CPC_Proof.sml` version-gates the cvc5 CPC rule vocabulary.
- `CPC_ProofParser.sml` treats CPC proof text as untrusted input; unknown
  constructs or rules fail with the cvc5 version in the diagnostic.
- `CPC_ProofReplay.sml` reconstructs supported steps through HOL kernel
  inferences and checked tactic certificates.

`CVC_ORACLE_TAC` and `CVC.CVC_SMT_Oracle` are outside this checked boundary.
For `CVC_TAC`, `UNSAT NONE` is not accepted: CPC replay must produce
`UNSAT (SOME thm)` and pass the original-goal and no-oracle checks.

Nonlinear real arithmetic replay may invoke CSDP through `SOSLib.REAL_SOS`.
CSDP is an untrusted semidefinite-solver dependency: it searches for SOS
certificates, while HOL checks the resulting certificate before any theorem is
accepted. If CSDP is unavailable, checked replay must fail with the explicit
dependency diagnostic rather than use an oracle or skip replay.

CI and regression gate:

The HolSmt selftest target is the CI gate for this boundary:

- `selftest.sml` checks that theorem-producing Z3 proof tests return the
  requested theorem and have no unexpected oracle or axiom tags.
- `Z3.sml` checks tags immediately after `Z3_SMT_Prover` replay.
- `Unittest.sml` contains deterministic regressions for the tag gate,
  parser/typechecker audit edges, and `sat`/`unknown` negative outcomes.

Any accepted `Z3_TAC` theorem with an unexpected oracle tag is a test failure.

Semantic mismatches and obligations:

The explicit list is generated in
`$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/SMTLIB_COVERAGE.md` under `Soundness Audit and
Semantic Mismatches`. Current manifest-backed audit items include:

- binder scoping, shadowing, quoted identifiers and local definitions;
- indexed and parametric sort reconstruction;
- SMT-LIB division-by-zero underspecification versus HOL encodings;
- bit-vector division, remainder, overflow, shift and rotation edge cases;
- FloatingPoint NaN, infinity and rounding-mode semantics;
- ArraysEx select/store versus current HOL function encoding;
- SMT-LIB datatype selectors are total but underspecified on constructors that
  do not own the selector. The HOL encoding chooses the `ARB` value for those
  branches, which is one concrete interpretation of the SMT-LIB semantics.
  An SMT `unsat` result holds for all such interpretations, so this HOL
  instance preserves checked replay soundness.
- `sat` and `unknown` solver results as non-theorem-producing outcomes.

For each item, accepted reconstruction needs a checked proof path, a
preprocessing theorem that relates the HOL and SMT-LIB meanings, or an explicit
unsupported diagnostic before replay. Silent acceptance or oracle fallback is
outside the audited boundary.

HOL-to-SMT translation has two regimes, selected by the P3.4 failure trigger
after preprocessing.  The byte-identical FirstOrder path encodes function
values as nested Array sorts, with select/store for application/update; terms
such as (P:'a -> bool) x remain supported there.  Shapes that path cannot
preserve, including surviving lambdas and non-symbol operators, select
HigherOrder.  Standard27 emits native SMT-LIB 2.7 HO-Core syntax, while
Z3LambdaArray lowers maps and application to nested Array/select and
eta-expands partially applied ranked constants.

The Z3 lowering is part of the trusted translation surface.  SMT-LIB 2.7
section 3.9 interprets map sorts as total maps, so a map `A -> B` and an
extensional Z3 `Array A B` have the same application and equality behavior:
map application becomes `select`, and nested maps become nested arrays.
Eta-expansion is sound independently of Z3: HOL proves
`f = (\x. f x)`, and the ranked-constant expansion used by the printer is
therefore HOL-side-provable.  The lowering introduces no axiom or oracle;
the solver proof must still replay to the original HOL goal and pass the
oracle-tag check.  Callers may override regime selection, while unsupported
residual shapes still fail deterministically.

Z3 version diagnostics and CI hooks:

Z3.sml reports the configured executable and discovered Z3 version through
configured_executable, configured_version and Z3version.  Z3_SMT_Prover adds
that version data path, together with the proof command stem, to proof parse
and replay failures before raising them to HolSmtLib.

Replay diagnostics from Z3_ProofReplay.sml include the proof rule, local proof
subterm, parsed HOL conclusion and a replay-state summary of asserted
hypotheses, definition hypotheses and Z3-created variables.  Unknown proof
rules are reported by Z3_ProofParser.sml with the missing rule name and local
proof subterm.

SmtResource.sml is the single source of truth for the checked FloatingPoint
bit-blast budget.  Z3 proof text is size-gated before Z3_ProofParser runs;
the checked add/sub and Float16 multiplication-identity circuit rungs use the
same module's term-size and per-step time caps.  The direct Float16
`fp.mul x 1.0 = x` atom closes through the checked symbolic-arithmetic rung;
the generated fpa2bv replay currently reaches that rung and step-time gates.
Float32 symbolic multiplication term-size gates before circuit expansion.
These incremental cases do not change the manifest-level FloatingPoint
support row above.  A breach starts with `resource-gated: fp-bitblast;`,
carries a `resource-gate:FloatingPoint:<case>` feature, and the command-line
driver reports `Z3_TAC_RESOURCE_GATED` rather than a replay failure.

Z3 th-lemma replay coverage:

  Theory      Collected subkind shapes       Replay status
  arith       farkas, assign-bounds,         checked by existing arith_prove path
              eq-propagate, gomory-cut,
              triangle-eq
  array       select-store, extensionality   checked by shared array_prove ladder
  bv          bit-blast                      checked by existing word/BBLAST path
  basic       eq-propagate                   checked by Boolean TAUT_PROVE, then
                                             arithmetic, bit-vector and array
                                             dispatch before METIS fallback
  seq/string  string equations, character    checked by SmtStringProve's
              bounds, regex membership,      symbolic, derivative and
              aut.accept transitions         proof-local rewrite ladders
  char        18-bit code-point bounds and   checked by word18 decomposition
              comparisons                    and HOL arithmetic
  regexp      string/regexp aliases for the  routed to the checked seq/string
              emitted seq/char families      prover; aliases are preserved
  fp          not observed on any supported  defensive route through the
              Z3 version; observed fpa2bv     checked SmtFpProve ladder;
              ground/atom/bit shapes          no occurrence is claimed

Indexed th-lemma failures include theory, subkind, remaining proof indices and
the parsed HOL conclusion in the replay error.

SMT-LIB conformance runner:

The local conformance suite is generated by:

  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --out /tmp/holsmt-conformance

The default suite requires no network access.  It generates parser/oracle/proof
smoke inputs for every logic represented by SmtLib_Logics.sml, plus small
metamorphic inputs for operand commutation, variable renaming, nested lets,
scoped assertions and a mixed array/integer case.  The runner writes
`conformance.json`, `CONFORMANCE.md`, proof-corpus JSON, raw Z3 stdout/stderr,
raw proofs and minimized failure repros under the output directory.

Large external benchmark runs are opt-in:

  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --out /tmp/holsmt-conformance \
    --benchmark-dir /path/to/smtlib-benchmarks \
    --z3-proof-dir /path/to/z3/proof/tests

Use `--logic` and repeated `--mode` flags to restrict a run.  The
typecheck-only and checked Z3_TAC modes default to HOL drivers built by:

  bin/Holmake -C src/HolSmt holsmt-typecheck
  bin/Holmake -C src/HolSmt holsmt-z3-tac

The typecheck driver accepts an SMT-LIB input path and logic name, runs the
`SmtLib_Parser` typechecker, and prints deterministic `TYPECHECK_PASS` or
`TYPECHECK_FAIL` records.  The checked Z3_TAC driver accepts the same arguments,
converts supported `check-sat` unsat scripts into a HOL goal over the parsed
assertions, proves it through the same `Z3.Z3_SMT_Prover` checked replay path
used by `HolSmtLib.Z3_TAC`, and rejects unexpected oracle/axiom tags.  Raw
query shapes outside that entry point, such as `get-model`, are reported as
explicit `Z3_TAC_UNSUPPORTED` diagnostics.  Custom drivers can still be
supplied with `--typecheck-command` or `--z3-tac-command`:

  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --mode typecheck-only \
    --mode z3-tac \
    --z3-tac-command 'my-z3-tac-check {input} {logic}'

If a default driver has not been built, that mode is reported as an explicit
unsupported environment condition.  Unconfigured hooks are counted as
`unsupported`, not hidden.  Unsupported counts are grouped by logic, mode,
SMT-LIB target version and discovered Z3 version in the generated reports.

Reproducing proof-replay failures:

  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --out /tmp/holsmt-replay-repro \
    --logic QF_UF \
    --mode proof-parse \
    --mode proof-replay \
    --z3 "${HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE:-z3}"

Failing modes preserve `input.smt2`, `result.json`, command output and raw Z3
proof text when available under:

  /tmp/holsmt-replay-repro/repro/<logic>/<case>/<mode>/

Replay the minimized or preserved input directly with the same proof mode.  For
Z3 4.x:

  "${HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE:-z3}" \
    proof=true pp.simplify_implies=false -smt2 \
    /tmp/holsmt-replay-repro/repro/<logic>/<case>/<mode>/input.smt2 \
    > /tmp/holsmt-replay-repro/manual.stdout \
    2> /tmp/holsmt-replay-repro/manual.stderr

Keep reducing the preserved `input.smt2` until the same unknown rule,
unsupported th-lemma shape or replay error remains, then record the minimized
input through the proof-corpus recorder:

  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/record_z3_proof_corpus.py \
    --out /tmp/holsmt-proof-corpus \
    --expected-rules $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/proof-corpus/minimal_expected_rules.json \
    --fail-on-unseen-rules \
    --fail-on-unknown-rules \
    /tmp/minimized-repro.smt2

The corpus output records Z3 version, input path, raw proof, rule histogram,
unseen rules, replay-unknown rules and malformed proof fragments.  Parser-only
wrappers such as `proof-bind` are reported separately from replay obligations.

Local CI contract commands:

  # Run the local contract checks below, resolving z3 from PATH when
  # HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE is not already set.  The script fails fast if that
  # executable is outside the exact tested anchor set; pass
  # --allow-unsupported-z3
  # to investigate proof-format drift intentionally.  The script also runs the
  # full five-anchor Z3 version matrix by default, using versioned
  # z3-* binaries discovered under ~/.local/bin or PATH when available.
  $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_holsmt_tests.sh

  # Download the exact tested Z3 anchors, run the
  # proof-corpus gate, checked replay smoke test and full selftest against
  # each executable, and write .holsmt-z3-version-matrix/report.json.  The
  # command exits nonzero unless every requested version passes.  Use
  # --z3 VERSION:PATH for locally installed versions when no compatible
  # official binary exists for the host.
  $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/verify_z3_versions.sh

  # Build representative Z3 source releases with TPTP support and install
  # versioned binaries such as ~/.local/bin/z3-4.11.2 and
  # ~/.local/bin/z3_tptp-4.11.2.
  $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/build_z3_versions.sh

  # Also run the Docker selftest matrix
  $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_holsmt_tests.sh --include-docker

  # Python tool unit tests
  python3 -m unittest \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/test_audit_coverage_manifest.py \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/test_generate_complete_corpus.py \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/test_generate_smtlib_coverage.py \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/test_record_z3_proof_corpus.py \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/test_run_conformance_suite.py

  # Coverage report regeneration and enforcing manifest audit
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/generate_smtlib_coverage.py --release
  git diff --exit-code -- \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/smtlib_coverage.json \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/SMTLIB_COVERAGE.md
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/audit_coverage_manifest.py --enforce

  # Parser conformance for the full checked-in corpus
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --out .holsmt-ci/conformance/parser \
    --no-default-suite \
    --corpus-dir $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/conformance-corpus/v1 \
    --mode parser-only

  # Z3 oracle, proof-parse and proof-replay conformance slice
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --out .holsmt-ci/conformance/z3 \
    --no-default-suite \
    --corpus-dir $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/conformance-corpus/v1 \
    --logic QF_UF \
    --logic QF_LIA \
    --mode z3-oracle \
    --mode proof-parse \
    --mode proof-replay \
    --timeout 20

  # Checked-in proof matrix and live unseen-rule gate
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/record_z3_proof_corpus.py \
    --validate-corpus-manifest
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/record_z3_proof_corpus.py \
    --out /tmp/holsmt-proof-corpus \
    --expected-rules $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/proof-corpus/minimal_expected_rules.json \
    --fail-on-unseen-rules \
    --fail-on-unknown-rules \
    $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/proof-corpus/minimal_bool_unsat.smt2

  # HOL driver conformance after building src/HolSmt drivers
  bin/Holmake -C src/HolSmt holsmt-typecheck holsmt-z3-tac
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --out .holsmt-ci/conformance/typecheck \
    --no-default-suite \
    --benchmark-dir $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/conformance/typecheck \
    --mode typecheck-only
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/run_conformance_suite.py \
    --out .holsmt-ci/conformance/z3-tac \
    --no-default-suite \
    --benchmark-dir $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/conformance/z3_tac \
    --mode z3-tac

  # Final contract audit against the executed conformance summaries
  python3 $HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/audit_coverage_manifest.py \
    --enforce \
    --conformance-report .holsmt-ci/conformance/parser/conformance.json \
    --conformance-report .holsmt-ci/conformance/z3/conformance.json \
    --conformance-report .holsmt-ci/conformance/typecheck/conformance.json \
    --conformance-report .holsmt-ci/conformance/z3-tac/conformance.json

  # SML unit tests without configured solvers, matching the CI Docker job
  docker buildx build \
    --load \
    --tag holsmt-nosolver:ci \
    --build-arg SML=poly \
    --build-arg 'BUILDOPTS=--no-cache --expk -j4' \
    -f developers/docker-ci/Dockerfile .
  docker run --rm \
    -e HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE= \
    -e HOL4_CVC_EXECUTABLE= \
    -e HOL4_YICES_EXECUTABLE= \
    holsmt-nosolver:ci \
    sh -lc 'src/HolSmt/selftest.exe'

  # Selected legacy Z3 Docker lanes.  The five-version verifier above is the
  # complete support contract.
  for z3_version in 4.12.4 4.13.0; do
    docker buildx build \
      --load \
      --tag "holsmt-z3:${z3_version}" \
      --build-arg SML=poly \
      --build-arg "Z3_VERSION=${z3_version}" \
      --build-arg 'BUILDOPTS=--no-cache --expk -j4' \
      -f developers/docker-ci/Dockerfile .
    docker run --rm "holsmt-z3:${z3_version}" \
      sh -lc '"$HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE" -version && src/HolSmt/selftest.exe'
  done

Adding support for a new Z3 proof rule:

  1. Add or minimize a corpus input that triggers the rule and run
     `record_z3_proof_corpus.py` with `--fail-on-unseen-rules` and
     `--fail-on-unknown-rules`.
  2. Extend `src/HolSmt/Z3_Proof.sml`: add a `proofterm` constructor if the
     rule needs a new replay shape, add the rule or alias to
     `proof_rule_registry`, and set the replay handler identity.
  3. Extend `src/HolSmt/Z3_ProofParser.sml` only when the rule needs a new
     premise shape, indexed metadata, alias normalization or wrapper behavior.
  4. Add the checked replay handler in `src/HolSmt/Z3_ProofReplay.sml`, or add
     an explicit unsupported diagnostic if the proof certificate does not yet
     expose enough evidence for sound replay.
  5. Add deterministic coverage in `src/HolSmt/Unittest.sml` and, when the raw
     proof extraction rules change, in
     `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/tests/test_record_z3_proof_corpus.py`.
  6. Update the expected proof-rule manifest or corpus fixture only after the
     parser/replay status is intentional.
  7. Update `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/smtlib_coverage.json` and
     `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/coverage_manifest.json`, regenerate
     `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/SMTLIB_COVERAGE.md`, and keep this README in agreement.

Updating the Z3 version matrix:

  1. If the support range changes, update `run_holsmt_tests.sh` and this README.
  2. Add representative exact versions to `verify_z3_versions.sh` and
     `build_z3_versions.sh`.
  3. Build the source releases with TPTP support and install the versioned
     binaries under `~/.local/bin`.
  4. Run `run_holsmt_tests.sh` or `verify_z3_versions.sh` so the matrix records
     the exact executables that passed the proof-corpus gate, checked replay
     smoke test and full HolSmt selftest.
  5. If proof options, aliases or unsupported diagnostics changed, update the
     proof-rule registry, replay tests and expected-rule manifests first.
  6. Update `$HOLSMT_VALIDATION_DIR/tools/coverage/smtlib_coverage.json` and the coverage
     manifest, regenerate the matrix, and adjust the "Supported Z3 versions"
     table above.

Pull-request CI retains PolyML expk Docker lanes for Z3 4.12.4 and 4.13.0.
These selected image lanes are not the full support contract; that contract is
the five-version verifier above.  The equivalent local hook is:

  docker buildx build \
    --build-arg SML=poly \
    --build-arg Z3_VERSION=<4.12.4|4.13.0> \
    --build-arg CVC_VERSION=5 \
    --build-arg CSDP_VERSION=1 \
    --build-arg 'BUILDOPTS=--no-cache --expk -j4 -t' \
    -f developers/docker-ci/Dockerfile .

The HolSmt selftest is part of that build.  `Z3_SMT_Prover` and selftest
theorem checks reject unexpected oracle/axiom tags on `Z3_TAC` outputs; `sat`
and `unknown` solver results are negative outcomes and must not produce HOL
theorems.

Further documentation can be found in the HOL Description (in
HOL/Manual/Description).
